        raster running) to run an RSID whose header play address is $0000 and
        which therefore installs its own IRQ/NMI handler. Measured over the whole
        collection: 3708 RSIDs and 109 PSIDs carry play=$0000; 2382 of them are
        silent on EVERY subtune, 1332 play normally and 103 are mixed. The silent
        ones are listed in data/misc/csid_silent_sids.txt and dropped from the
        catalog, so no unplayable row is ever shown.

Component: Compute!'s Sidplayer sequencer (.mus / .str) -- musplugin,
           shown in the app as "ChipMachine Clean Room SIDPlayer"
Author: Original code written for this project (clean room). The FORMAT is Craig
        Chamberlin's Compute!'s Sidplayer, published by COMPUTE! Publications.
        Implemented from two freely-distributed format descriptions shipped in
        the Compute's Gazette SID Collection: MUS_format_A.txt by Peter Weighill
        -- which states it was produced by hex-editing editor output "without any
        reference to the book written by Craig Chamberlin" -- and
        MUS_format_B.txt by Dick Thornton.
License: Our implementation is original work under this project's terms. The two
         format descriptions are freely distributed within the CGSC archive.
Notice: CLEAN ROOM. No code was taken or consulted from any other Sidplayer
        implementation, nor from any disassembly of the original 1980s Sidplayer
        routine. Behaviour the documents leave unspecified was derived by
        black-box observation of emulator register OUTPUT only. The SID chip
        emulation underneath is cSID (WTFPL, above). See musplugin/README.md for
        the full derivation.
        METHOD, for the record: the only thing observed was the stream of SID
        register writes an emulator produces while playing a file -- address,
        value and cycle timestamp. That output was compared against ours on a
        1 ms grid and the sequencer's rules were fitted to the difference. No
        player code, symbol, table or disassembly listing was read at any point;
        where a rule could not be recovered from output alone it was left
        unimplemented and recorded as a known gap rather than guessed at from
        source. Thirteen behaviours the documents do not specify were recovered
        this way (tick rate, note table rounding, default tempo, startup delay,
        repeat-count semantics, explicit note lengths, zero-length events,
        filter-tracking, filter register scope, the modulation schedule, the two
        sweeps and portamento).
        The corpus-wide validation results are recorded with the plugin.

